  <data name="SettingsPlaybackControlsToggle.Header" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Show playback controls in Clasp</value>
    <comment>Label of a toggle switch in Settings &gt; Sound. One line, up to ~50 characters visible (wraps if longer). Shows Play/Pause/Previous/Next in the Clasp panel.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="SettingsPlaybackControlsNote.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Shows what's playing in Windows, with Play, Pause, Previous and Next, in the Clasp panel. Turning this off stops Clasp from reading Windows media.</value>
    <comment>Explanation text under a control in Settings &gt; Sound. Wraps over several lines; about 2 to 4 lines at ~480 px wide.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="SettingsPauseOnRemovalToggle.Header" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Pause media when AirPods are removed</value>
    <comment>Label of a toggle switch in Settings &gt; Sound. One line, up to ~50 characters visible (wraps if longer). Another note quotes this label by name: keep it identical to the quote in SettingsEarDetectionNote.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="SettingsPauseOnRemovalNote.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Clasp pauses when you take your AirPods out and resumes only what it paused. It cancels the resume if you use playback controls, switch players or disconnect. This doesn't change the ear detection built into your AirPods. It needs ear changes from your AirPods, or "Ear detection (nearby estimate)" turned on in AirPods.</value>
    <comment>Explanation text under a control in Settings &gt; Sound. Wraps over several lines; about 2 to 4 lines at ~480 px wide. Contains the quoted name 'Ear detection (nearby estimate)': keep it identical to SettingsEarDetectionToggle.Header. The final 'AirPods' refers to the AirPods section of this window.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="SettingsPhoneAudioHeading.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Phone audio</value>
    <comment>Subheading inside the Sound section of the Settings page. One line, short.</comment>
  </data>
