    <comment>Label of a toggle switch in Settings &gt; Shortcuts. One line, up to ~50 characters visible (wraps if longer). 'clasp://' is a literal link scheme, do not translate it.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="SettingsAllowLinksNote.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Lets other apps on this PC, like Stream Deck or PowerToys, ask Clasp to connect, switch sound or open settings by opening a link such as clasp://connect. Links can only do the things listed below. Turning this on adds a small entry for your Windows account only, and turning it off removes it.</value>
    <comment>Explanation text under a control in Settings &gt; Shortcuts. Wraps over several lines; about 2 to 4 lines at ~480 px wide. Mentions third-party app names (Stream Deck, PowerToys) and the example link 'clasp://connect': keep those as is.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="SettingsLinksWebsiteNote.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Websites can open these links too. Clasp asks before connecting, changing your sound, fixing the connection or playing or pausing your media.</value>
    <comment>Explanation text under a control in Settings &gt; Shortcuts. Wraps over several lines; about 2 to 4 lines at ~480 px wide.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="SettingsGlobalShortcutsHeading.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Global shortcuts</value>
    <comment>Subheading inside the Shortcuts section of the Settings page. One line, short.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="SettingsGlobalShortcutsNote.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Shortcuts work in any app and are empty until you set them. Use Ctrl, Alt or Win, for example Ctrl+Alt+A, then press Enter or Tab to save. Windows keeps some shortcuts for itself.</value>
    <comment>Explanation text under a control in Settings &gt; Shortcuts. Wraps over several lines; about 2 to 4 lines at ~480 px wide. Contains key names (Ctrl, Alt, Win, Enter, Tab) and the example 'Ctrl+Alt+A'; use the key names as printed on a keyboard in the target language.</comment>
  </data>
